1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,290
On Friday, 28 April 1967, in Houston, Texas,

2
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:19,330
the heavyweight champion of the world was called by name for induction into the United States Army,

3
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:21,170
and he did not step forward.

4
00:00:21,667 --> 00:00:25,237
He was twenty-five years old, he had never lost a professional fight,

5
00:00:25,947 --> 00:00:28,037
and he was one of the most famous men on earth.

6
00:00:28,467 --> 00:00:32,077
He was born Cassius Marcellus Clay Junior in Louisville, Kentucky,

7
00:00:32,507 --> 00:00:37,657
on 17 January 1942, and he started boxing as a boy of twelve.

8
00:00:38,233 --> 00:00:43,443
At eighteen he won the light heavyweight gold medal at the 1960 Olympic Games in Rome.

9
00:00:43,995 --> 00:00:50,310
On 25 February 1964, in Miami Beach, he beat the champion Sonny Liston,

10
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:52,570
who did not answer the bell for the seventh round.

11
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:53,970
Ten days later,

12
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:57,770
the Nation of Islam leader Elijah Muhammad announced on the radio

13
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:00,890
that the new champion would be called Muhammad Ali.

14
00:01:01,462 --> 00:01:05,217
In the thirteen months before Houston he defended his title seven times,

15
00:01:05,427 --> 00:01:06,577
in five different cities,

16
00:01:07,027 --> 00:01:11,517
and his last fight had been on 22 March 1967, five weeks earlier.

17
00:01:12,028 --> 00:01:14,023
The army, meanwhile, had passed him over:

18
00:01:14,453 --> 00:01:19,143
in 1964 he had failed its qualifying test, because of his spelling and writing.

19
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:21,250
Then the war in Vietnam grew.

20
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:25,390
In November 1965 the army lowered its test standards,

21
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:31,070
and in February 1966 Ali was reclassified 1-A, which meant he could be drafted.

22
00:01:37,395 --> 00:01:40,370
He said publicly that he had no quarrel with the Viet Cong,

23
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:41,950
and that he would not serve.

24
00:01:50,095 --> 00:01:52,510
This is the story of what that refusal cost him,

25
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:54,930
and how it ended in a courtroom in Washington.

26
00:02:00,662 --> 00:02:05,357
In 1967 the United States was drafting young men for a war in Vietnam

27
00:02:05,367 --> 00:02:06,977
that was growing every year.

28
00:02:07,428 --> 00:02:12,023
When Ali was reclassified, he applied to be treated as a conscientious objector,

29
00:02:12,413 --> 00:02:14,943
a person who refuses to fight because of conscience.

30
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:17,630
His local draft board denied the claim,

31
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:20,850
and it went on to the Department of Justice and the FBI.

32
00:02:21,595 --> 00:02:23,530
His case rested on his faith.

33
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:24,770
Ali said

34
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:30,070
that the Quran teaches Muslims not to take part in a war unless Allah himself declares it.

35
00:02:37,662 --> 00:02:41,537
A hearing officer, Judge Lawrence Grauman, looked into the claim,

36
00:02:41,947 --> 00:02:45,277
found Ali sincere, and recommended that it be granted.

37
00:02:45,895 --> 00:02:47,470
The Department of Justice disagreed.

38
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:50,910
It advised that he was not opposed to war in any form,

39
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:53,050
that his objection was not really religious,

40
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:56,050
and that he had not shown his sincerity in good time.

41
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:03,270
On 20 February 1967 the Kentucky appeal board voted four to nothing to reject his claim,

42
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:04,730
and it gave no reason.

43
00:03:05,315 --> 00:03:06,870
So the draft system had decided,

44
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:10,890
and on 28 April he reported to the induction station in Houston,

45
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:11,810
with his lawyers.

46
00:03:12,467 --> 00:03:16,337
There, an army officer called his name. Ali did not move.

47
00:03:16,847 --> 00:03:18,417
The officer called his birth name,

48
00:03:18,707 --> 00:03:21,417
Cassius Clay, and still he stayed where he was.

49
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,150
In all, his names were called three times, and he did not step forward.

50
00:03:26,633 --> 00:03:30,663
He told the officers he was claiming exemption as a minister of the religion of Islam.

51
00:03:31,338 --> 00:03:33,343
The other draftees left through a back door,

52
00:03:33,773 --> 00:03:35,803
while Ali walked out the front to face the reporters.

53
00:03:36,467 --> 00:03:38,597
The boxing authorities then moved against him:

54
00:03:38,967 --> 00:03:41,757
the New York State Athletic Commission suspended his licence,

55
00:03:42,267 --> 00:03:44,897
and the World Boxing Association took away his title.

56
00:03:45,462 --> 00:03:49,937
Other commissions followed, and on 8 May a federal grand jury indicted him.

57
00:03:50,447 --> 00:03:52,517
Now the fight was no longer in a ring.

58
00:03:58,038 --> 00:04:00,363
The trial was held in Houston, and it was short.

59
00:04:00,962 --> 00:04:07,297
On 20 June 1967 a jury of six men and six women, all of them white,

60
00:04:07,927 --> 00:04:10,997
took twenty-one minutes to find him guilty of refusing induction.

61
00:04:11,623 --> 00:04:14,143
The judge gave him the most the law allowed:

62
00:04:14,153 --> 00:04:17,623
five years in prison and a fine of ten thousand dollars.

63
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:27,010
Ali did not go to prison. He paid a bond,

64
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:28,990
and stayed free while his lawyers appealed.

65
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:32,410
But boxing was closed to him, and it stayed closed for three years.

66
00:04:33,028 --> 00:04:35,743
He travelled the country speaking for the Nation of Islam.

67
00:04:36,373 --> 00:04:42,163
On 25 June 1967 a San Diego television crew filmed him surrounded by a crowd.

68
00:04:42,695 --> 00:04:46,390
According to the station, the crowd turned angry on its news crew,

69
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:48,950
and Ali stepped in to protect the reporters.

70
00:04:55,627 --> 00:04:57,617
Three days later, on 28 June,

71
00:04:57,767 --> 00:05:00,477
a reporter there asked him about the draft and his religion.

72
00:05:01,062 --> 00:05:04,817
Without a crowd around him, the station noted, he was very subdued.

73
00:05:05,462 --> 00:05:07,897
Another former heavyweight champion, Joe Louis,

74
00:05:08,167 --> 00:05:11,757
was filmed in 1967 talking about Ali and the draft.

75
00:05:17,428 --> 00:05:22,783
On 6 May 1968 the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals upheld the conviction.

76
00:05:23,462 --> 00:05:27,897
In 1969 the Supreme Court sent the case back on a separate point,

77
00:05:27,947 --> 00:05:32,117
and in 1970 the appeals court upheld the conviction again.

78
00:05:32,733 --> 00:05:38,803
All this time Ali was out of the ring, from March 1967 to October 1970,

79
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:41,183
about three years and seven months.

80
00:05:41,953 --> 00:05:46,103
In 1970 a court ordered New York to restore his boxing licence.

81
00:05:46,728 --> 00:05:51,183
On 26 October 1970 he came back and beat Jerry Quarry,

82
00:05:51,373 --> 00:05:53,323
in a fight stopped after three rounds.

83
00:05:58,867 --> 00:06:03,057
On 8 March 1971, at Madison Square Garden in New York,

84
00:06:03,447 --> 00:06:07,077
he fought Joe Frazier, and lost, for the first time as a professional.

85
00:06:07,633 --> 00:06:10,263
The Supreme Court heard his case six weeks later,

86
00:06:10,533 --> 00:06:13,243
on 19 April, in about an hour of argument.

87
00:06:20,433 --> 00:06:24,063
The government's lawyers argued that his opposition to war was selective,

88
00:06:24,333 --> 00:06:27,803
because members of the Nation of Islam did not oppose a holy war.

89
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:38,970
On 28 June 1971, four years and two months after Houston,

90
00:06:39,580 --> 00:06:41,410
the Court ruled eight to nothing in his favour.

91
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:46,070
It was a per curiam decision, which means no single justice signed it.

92
00:06:46,462 --> 00:06:50,757
Justice Thurgood Marshall took no part, because he had been Solicitor General,

93
00:06:51,007 --> 00:06:53,037
the government's lawyer, when the case began.

94
00:06:53,628 --> 00:06:54,883
The government itself admitted

95
00:06:54,893 --> 00:06:57,703
that two of the Justice Department's three reasons were wrong.

96
00:06:58,233 --> 00:07:00,623
And because the appeal board had given no reason,

97
00:07:01,073 --> 00:07:04,883
nobody could tell which reason it had used, so the conviction could not stand.

98
00:07:05,433 --> 00:07:07,463
The Court ruled on that flaw in the process,

99
00:07:07,813 --> 00:07:09,963
not on whether Ali's belief was sincere,

100
00:07:10,273 --> 00:07:12,323
and Justice William Douglas wrote separately

101
00:07:12,333 --> 00:07:13,863
that he would have freed him outright.

102
00:07:14,495 --> 00:07:15,790
Behind closed doors,

103
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:20,870
according to a 1979 book by the journalists Bob Woodward and Scott Armstrong,

104
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:25,190
the justices had first voted five to three to uphold the conviction.

105
00:07:25,867 --> 00:07:27,317
One of them, John Harlan,

106
00:07:27,607 --> 00:07:31,857
is said to have changed his mind after reading about the teachings of the Nation of Islam.

107
00:07:32,433 --> 00:07:33,983
With only eight justices voting,

108
00:07:34,553 --> 00:07:37,043
a four to four tie would have left the conviction standing.

109
00:07:43,933 --> 00:07:46,143
Ali was free, and he went back to the ring.

110
00:07:46,993 --> 00:07:52,163
On 30 October 1974, in Kinshasa, in what was then Zaire,

111
00:07:52,433 --> 00:07:53,903
at the age of thirty-two,

112
00:07:53,913 --> 00:07:55,943
he knocked out George Foreman in the eighth round

113
00:07:55,953 --> 00:07:57,603
and won the heavyweight title back.

114
00:07:58,095 --> 00:08:01,470
He became the first heavyweight to win the world title three separate times,

115
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:06,290
and in 1996 he lit the flame at the opening of the Olympic Games in Atlanta.

116
00:08:06,833 --> 00:08:10,463
He had been sentenced to five years in prison, and he never served a day of it.

117
00:08:10,833 --> 00:08:12,903
What he lost was his title, his licence,

118
00:08:13,193 --> 00:08:15,183
and nearly four years at the peak of his career.

119
00:08:16,233 --> 00:08:20,203
The Court never said he was right. Would you have stepped forward?

